<title>GERMANY: ZEITLER SEES GOOD CHANCE OF GERMANY QUALIFYING FOR EMU.</title>
<headline>ZEITLER SEES GOOD CHANCE OF GERMANY QUALIFYING FOR EMU.</headline>
<dateline>MUNICH 1997-01-20</dateline>
<text>
<p>Bundesbank council member Franz-Christoph Zeitler said despite Germany's relatively high new public borrowing in 1996, he saw a good chance that it would qualify for monetary union.</p>
<p>Zeitler, who is also president of the state central bank of Bavaria, told Bayerischer Rundfunk in a Sunday interview that fulfilling the convergence criteria took precedence over sticking to the timetable and trying to get as many states as possible into the planned currency union.</p>
<p>&quot;The principle remains that quality must have priority over the other aims,&quot; Zeitler said.</p>
<p>He also said he saw no reason to expect an increase in interest rates linked to the launch of European monetary union, scheduled for January 1, 1999.</p>
</text>
